        "Which engine speaks this agent's replies aloud when the user has spoken replies switched on. Same four values and the same no-silent-fallback rule as the dictation engine, and a separate setting because the risks are not symmetric: dictation sends the user's own words to the engine, while synthesis sends the agent's reply — which may quote the record even when the user said nothing sensitive.": "Which engine speaks this agent's replies aloud when the user has spoken replies switched on. Same four values and the same no-silent-fallback rule as the dictation engine, and a separate setting because the risks are not symmetric: dictation sends the user's own words to the engine, while synthesis sends the agent's reply — which may quote the record even when the user said nothing sensitive.",
        "Which engine turns speech into text for this agent. NOT the same axis as talkEnabled, which is about Nextcloud Talk rooms; this is about microphones. 'browser' uses the browser's own speech recognition: instant, live, and in Chrome it streams the microphone to Google's servers — fine for a shopping list, wrong for a case file. 'local' uses this instance's speech service: private, and slower (measured ~3s per sentence on CPU with faster-whisper-base, ~80s with the large model). 'auto' prefers the browser and falls back to local where the browser has no speech API at all, which is every Firefox user. 'off' offers no microphone. A 'local' agent NEVER falls back to the browser, even when the speech service is down — silently sending confidential audio to a cloud engine is the failure this setting exists to prevent.": "Which engine turns speech into text for this agent. NOT the same axis as talkEnabled, which is about Nextcloud Talk rooms; this is about microphones. 'browser' uses the browser's own speech recognition: instant, live, and in Chrome it streams the microphone to Google's servers — fine for a shopping list, wrong for a case file. 'local' uses this instance's speech service: private, and slower (measured ~3s per sentence on CPU with faster-whisper-base, ~80s with the large model). 'auto' prefers the browser and falls back to local where the browser has no speech API at all, which is every Firefox user. 'off' offers no microphone. A 'local' agent NEVER falls back to the browser, even when the speech service is down — silently sending confidential audio to a cloud engine is the failure this setting exists to prevent.",
